[# CX in Practice: A Field Guide]

[p By [a href=https://github.com/ardec Erik Paulson] · [date 2026-04-20]]

[p CX is a [** structured] format that looks like brackets and reads like
  natural text. This post walks through a typical working session.]

[## Setting Up]

[p Build from source — the whole toolchain compiles with a single command:]

[``` lang=bash [|
git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx
make build   # builds libcx and the cx CLI
make test    # runs all 9 language binding test suites
|]]

[## Your First Document]

[p Start with a simple config. Sized types pin numeric ranges, boolean
   sigils replace explicit [` flag=true] and [` flag=false], and line
   comments document intent inline:]

[``` lang=cx [#
[server host=localhost +tls -debug    # +tls -> tls=true; -debug -> debug=false
  [port :u16 8080]                    # u16 covers 0..65535
  [max_request_bytes :u32 1_048_576]  # 1 MiB - underscores improve readability
  [tags :string[] web api internal]
]
#]]

[## Converting Formats]

[p Pipe any CX file through the [` cx] CLI or call the library directly:]

[ul
  [li [` cx --json config.cx] — compact JSON for APIs]
  [li [` cx --xml  config.cx] — XML for legacy systems]
  [li [` cx --toml config.cx] — TOML for human editors]
  [li [` cx --md   doc.cx]    — Markdown for documentation]
]

[## Mixed Content]

[p CX handles documents and data in the same syntax. Inline markup works
  anywhere: [** bold], [* italic], [` code], [~~ strikethrough].]

[> [p The goal was one format you never have to escape from.]]

[## What's Next]

[p See the [a href=https://cxhome.org/docs full documentation] for
  language binding guides, the conformance suite, and the grammar spec.]
